{
	"Name": "Okta - User Aggregates",
	"Description": "Schedule: every hour at minute 17 over the last 25 hours (scheduled search \"Okta - User Aggregates\").\n\nEvery hour, rebuilds the Okta user-directory aggregates and saves them to the resource okta_user_aggregates (columns ts,metric,key,value,rowkey). @users takes the newest record per user from the last 25 hours of $OKTA_USERS (which always includes the hosted ingester's daily full user snapshot); one inner query per metric counts them (status, user type, division, department, job title, manager, timezone, credential provider type and name, last sign-in and password age of active users, average password age, average days from created to activated); the main query stacks the results with dump -p and writes them with table -save -update rowkey. Last sign-in comes from the System Log ($OKTA), not from the user records, whose lastLogin is only current when the user record was last written: @signins takes the newest successful user.session.start per user (actor.id) in the run's window, @user_last_signin keeps the newer of that and the stored value, and the result is saved as user_last_signin rows (rowkey user_last_signin|<user id>), so users who did not sign in during the window keep their last sign-in. \"never\" means no successful sign-in since the resource started tracking; run the saved query manually over a long range (for example 365 days) to seed it.\n\nrowkey is metric|key for snapshot rows and ts|metric|key for the last_signin_history and password_age_history rows. -update replaces resource rows whose rowkey is in the new results and keeps every other row, so the history grows by one point per run and is never pruned, and a snapshot key that no longer occurs (a department nobody is in any more, a status or bucket that dropped to zero) keeps its last count. A run that finds no users changes nothing.\n\nThe Okta User Overview and User Authentication Overview dashboards and the timezone table on Okta Sign-In Locations read only that resource (dump -r); detections never read it. Needs the hosted ingester's daily full user snapshot to cover users who have not changed, and its owner needs permission to write resources.",
